window.MTH105_INDEX=[{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#statistics","n":"What is statistics?","t":"What is statistics?","x":"Statistics is the science of collecting, organizing, analyzing, interpreting and communicating data to support decisions. A transport authority wants to know whether a new Metro feeder bus is worth running. It counts riders (collect), puts them in a table (organize), draws a chart (analyze), reads it (interpret) and reports a decision (communicate). Data: illustrative. Every statistics question follows the same five steps. This course teaches each one."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#descriptive","n":"Descriptive and inferential statistics","t":"Descriptive and inferential statistics","x":"Descriptive statistics describe the data we have: tables, graphs, averages. Inferential statistics use a sample to say something about the whole population. Sort each statement. Ask: does it stay with the data we have, or go beyond it? Weeks 1–6 are mostly descriptive. From Week 10, we use samples to estimate the population: inferential statistics."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#population","n":"Population and sample","t":"Population and sample","x":"The population is the whole group we study (size N). A sample is the part we measure (size n). A number describing the population is a parameter; a number from a sample is a statistic. A survey of 250 Dubai residents finds that 85 use the Metro. The 250 are the sample; all Dubai residents are the population. Data: illustrative. We use the statistic (p̂ = 34%) to estimate the unknown parameter p: the true share of all residents."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#types","n":"Types of data","t":"Types of data","x":"Qualitative data are categories: nominal (no order) or ordinal (ordered). Quantitative data are numbers: discrete (counted) or continuous (measured). Classify these everyday variables. The type decides the tools: categories → counts, bar and pie charts, mode. Numbers → histograms, mean, spread."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#samples","n":"Representative samples and sample size","t":"Representative samples and sample size","x":"A representative sample looks like the population. Choose it at random, not by convenience. Larger random samples give estimates closer to the truth. A newsletter asks 30 students in the library at 9 PM; 21 say they prefer studying at night. Headline: “70% of students prefer night study.” The sample is biased: students in the library at night already study at night. Drag the sample size. With small n, sample results swing widely; with large n, they cluster near the true value."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#outliers","n":"The impact of outliers","t":"The impact of outliers","x":"An outlier is a value far from the rest of the data. Pizza delivery times (min): 12, 14, 13, 15, 46. One late delivery (46) is far from the others. The outlier pulls the mean up to 20; the median stays at 14. With outliers, report the median."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#frequency","n":"Frequency and relative frequency","t":"Frequency and relative frequency","x":"The frequency f is how many times a category occurs. The relative frequency rf is its share of the total: f ÷ n. 40 students record how they get to campus: car 16, Metro 12, bus 8, walk 4. Data: illustrative. For categories, a bar chart compares sizes; a pie chart shows parts of a whole (100%)."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#classes","n":"Grouping numbers into classes","t":"Grouping numbers into classes","x":"Many numbers are grouped into classes of equal width. Each class has limits, boundaries (± 0.5) and a midpoint. 25 test scores range from 42 to 98. Listing all 25 shows little; six classes show the pattern. Build the frequency table: class, boundaries, midpoint, f, rf and cumulative frequency. Type your own data."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#histogram","n":"Histograms and shape","t":"Histograms and shape","x":"A histogram draws a frequency table as touching bars, one per class. Its shape is symmetric, right-skewed or left-skewed. Ages of 30 visitors to a science museum: most are 10–39, a few are older. Data: illustrative. Follow the tail. Tail to the right: positively skewed. Tail to the left: negatively skewed. No long tail: symmetric."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#polygon","n":"Frequency polygon and ogive","t":"Frequency polygon and ogive","x":"A frequency polygon joins the class midpoints. An ogive plots cumulative frequency at each upper boundary. From the museum data: how many visitors were younger than 49.5? The ogive answers directly: 26 of 30. The polygon shows the shape. The ogive answers “how many below a value?”."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#mean","n":"The mean","t":"The mean","x":"The mean x̄ is the balance point of the data: add all values and divide by how many there are. Five friends sleep 8, 9, 10, 12 and 13 hours over a weekend. What is the typical amount? Data: illustrative. If all five slept the same amount, each would sleep 10.4 hours. The mean uses every value, so one extreme value moves it."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#median","n":"Median and mode","t":"Median and mode","x":"The median is the middle value after sorting. The mode is the most frequent value; it is the only centre for nominal data. Delivery times (min): 7, 3, 9, 5, 20, 6. Sort first: 3, 5, 6, 7, 9, 20. Type any data to see the middle and the mode."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#which","n":"Which centre to use?","t":"Which centre to use?","x":"In symmetric data, mean ≈ median ≈ mode. Skew pulls the mean towards the tail. Salaries in a small company: most earn 42–62 thousand, three managers earn 150–200 thousand. Data: Week 1–2 slides. For skewed data or outliers, report the median. For symmetric data, the mean."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#quartiles","n":"Percentiles and quartiles","t":"Percentiles and quartiles","x":"A percentile gives the position of a value: P25 = Q1, P50 = median, P75 = Q3. “Your score is at the 75th percentile” means 75% of the class scored at or below you. Sort, find the median, then the median of each half. When n is odd, leave the median out of both halves."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#range-iqr","n":"Range, IQR and the boxplot","t":"Range, IQR and the boxplot","x":"The range is max − min. The IQR (interquartile range) is Q3 − Q1: the spread of the middle 50%. Two bus routes both average 30 minutes. On one, trips take 28–32 min; on the other, 15–60 min. Same mean, very different reliability. Data: illustrative. The boxplot draws the five-number summary: min, Q1, median, Q3, max. A long whisker or a dot beyond the fences shows skew or outliers."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#variance","n":"Sample variance","t":"Sample variance","x":"The variance s² is the average squared distance from the mean. For a sample, divide by n − 1. Five students’ quiz scores: 4, 6, 7, 9, 14. Mean = 8. How far are they from 8, on average? Variance is in squared units (points²), so it is hard to read. That is why we take its square root next."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#sd","n":"Standard deviation","t":"Standard deviation","x":"The standard deviation s is the square root of the variance: the typical distance from the mean, in the original units. Same quiz scores: s² = 14.5 points². A typical score is about 3.8 points from the mean of 8. Larger s = more spread. Check any data set step by step with the deviation table."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#cv","n":"Coefficient of variation","t":"Coefficient of variation","x":"The coefficient of variation (CV) is the standard deviation as a percentage of the mean. City A temperature: mean 15 °C, s 6 °C. City B rainfall: mean 100 mm, s 30 mm. Different units: which varies more? Data: illustrative. CV has no units, so it compares spread across different means or units. Smaller CV = more consistent. Here temperature varies more, relative to its mean."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#sample-space","n":"Sample space and events","t":"Sample space and events","x":"An experiment has uncertain results; each result is an outcome. All outcomes form the sample space S. An event is a set of outcomes. Roll one die: S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. “Even number” is the event {2, 4, 6}. Before calculating any probability, list S and the outcomes in the event."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#basic-rule","n":"The basic rule","t":"The basic rule","x":"When outcomes are equally likely, P(E) = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes. Every probability is between 0 and 1. A bag holds 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green balls. Pick one without looking. Data: illustrative. 0.5 means red comes up in about half of many repeated picks. All outcomes together add up to 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#complement","n":"The complement rule","t":"The complement rule","x":"“Not A” is everything outside A. P(A) and P(not A) always add to 1. 20% of deliveries arrive late. What share arrives on time? Data: illustrative. Use the complement when “not A” or “at least one” is easier to count the other way round."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#addition","n":"The addition rule (OR)","t":"The addition rule (OR)","x":"“A or B” means at least one happens. Add, then subtract the overlap so it is not counted twice. If A and B are mutually exclusive (no overlap), just add. Of 60 students, 35 take Arabic, 25 take French and 10 take both. Data: illustrative. In a Venn diagram, the overlap sits inside both circles. Change the counts and watch the rule."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#multiplication","n":"The multiplication rule (AND) and tree diagrams","t":"The multiplication rule (AND) and tree diagrams","x":"For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). In a tree, multiply along a path; add paths for “or”. A student is assigned a group (Male or Female) and a lab session (Morning or Evening) at random. Each split is 1/2. The tree lists the sample space: 2 × 2 = 4 equally likely paths. P(Evening) = 1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2 (add the paths)."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#event-types","n":"Types of events","t":"Types of events","x":"Mutually exclusive: cannot happen together. Independent: one does not change the other. Dependent: one changes the other. Complementary: opposites that add to 1. Name the relationship for each pair. The relationship decides the rule: OR → add (minus the overlap); AND → multiply; NOT → 1 minus."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#random-variable","n":"Discrete random variables and valid distributions","t":"Discrete random variables and valid distributions","x":"A random variable X gives a number to each outcome. Discrete: countable values (0, 1, 2 …). A distribution is valid when every P(x) is between 0 and 1 and all P(x) add to 1. X = number of buses arriving at a stop in one hour. Data: illustrative. Edit the probabilities and the tool checks both conditions."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#expected-value","n":"Expected value E(X)","t":"Expected value E(X)","x":"The expected value E(X), or μ, is the long-run average of X: multiply each value by its probability and add. Same bus stop: 0, 1, 2 or 3 buses with probabilities 0.1, 0.3, 0.4, 0.2. Over many hours, the stop gets 1.7 buses per hour on average. E(X) need not be a possible value of X."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#variance-x","n":"Variance and standard deviation of X","t":"Variance and standard deviation of X","x":"Var(X) measures how far X typically lands from E(X). SD = √Var(X), in the units of X. Same bus stop, with μ = 1.7. In a typical hour, the number of buses is about 0.9 away from the average of 1.7. Bigger SD = less predictable."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Five parts, 50 exercises","x":"Work through the five parts in order. Answer on paper, then open the model answer. Method: as covered in class and in last Wednesday’s lab revision."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s1","n":"1 · Draw graphs","t":"Part 1A · Draw the graph","x":"Histogram: boundaries on the x-axis, touching bars. Polygon: midpoints, closed on the axis. Boxplot: min, Q1, median, Q3, max."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s2","n":"1 · Read graphs","t":"Part 1B · Read the graph","x":"Read the values first, then calculate."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s3","n":"2 · Centre","t":"Part 2A · Central tendency","x":"Mean, median, mode. Sort the data before the median."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s4","n":"2 · Spread","t":"Part 2B · Measures of variation","x":"Range, IQR, variance, SD, CV. For s²: subtract, square, add, divide by n − 1."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s5","n":"3 · The rules","t":"Part 3 · Probability rules","x":"AND means multiply. OR means add; if BOTH is given, subtract it once. NOT means 1 minus."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s6","n":"3 · Simple and NOT","t":"Part 3A · Simple probability and complement","x":"Count favourable over total. For NOT, subtract from 1."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s7","n":"3 · OR","t":"Part 3B · Addition rule (OR)","x":"No overlap: just add. BOTH given: add, then subtract BOTH. Fill the Venn diagram from the middle out."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s8","n":"3 · AND","t":"Part 3C · Multiplication rule (AND)","x":"Independent and fair: every choice of two is 1/2. Multiply."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s9","n":"4 · Tree diagrams","t":"Part 4 · Tree diagrams","x":"Every step splits into two equal branches of 1/2. Multiply along a path; add paths for OR."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s10","n":"5 · Valid and k","t":"Part 5A · Valid distribution and k","x":"Every P(x) between 0 and 1, and the sum equals 1."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s11","n":"5 · E, Var, SD","t":"Part 5B · E(X), Var(X) and SD","x":"E(X) = Σx·P(x). Var(X) = Σ(x − μ)²·P(x). SD = √Var(X). Interpret in one sentence."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s12","n":"Exam day","t":"Exam day","x":"Monday 19 October 2026, 9:30 AM, HUB-Yard Zones A, B, D. Bring your ID and a standard calculator. Good luck."}];
